Make it possible to CPU-offload a Qwen3-VL vision tower's transformer blocks
to pinned host RAM (UVA zero-copy), which stock vLLM 0.27.1 cannot do.

MEASURED VERDICT (2026-08-23, this box): it works and recovers 0.78 GiB of KV
pool, but costs 2.6-3.8x on image-encode latency. Default is OFF (0). Turn it
on only if GPU KV blocks are worth more to you than image latency -- see the
numbers at the bottom before deciding.

Why stock vLLM can't do this: --cpu-offload-gb / --cpu-offload-params are
accepted without error but are a silent NO-OP for vision towers. The
offloader's only call site in the entire codebase is
`get_offloader().wrap_modules(...)` inside make_layers()
(model_executor/models/utils.py), and make_layers() builds the *language
model* decoder stack only (qwen3_5.py's Qwen3_5Model). The vision tower builds
its blocks with a plain `nn.ModuleList` (qwen3_vl.py's
Qwen3_VisionTransformer), so the offloader never sees them. Symptom: no
"Total CPU offloaded parameters" line is logged and the KV pool shrinks by the
full weight of the tower. Confirmed by grep: `wrap_modules` appears at exactly
one call site.

Note the *filter* would have worked -- "visual" is segment-exact (the matcher
is `f".{param}." in f".{name}."`) and matches exactly the 333 model.visual.*
tensors and zero language-model tensors on this checkpoint. It is only the
call-site coverage that is missing.

What this patch adds:

  VLLM_VISION_CPU_OFFLOAD_GB (float, default 0 = off, upstream behavior)

When > 0, Qwen3_VisionTransformer routes its blocks through a *dedicated*
UVAOffloader instance with that byte budget. A dedicated instance rather than
the global one, so this budget can neither be consumed by nor steal budget
from language-model layers: cpu_offload_params is left empty (= "offload
everything handed to me") and the only modules handed over are the ViT blocks.

Mechanically (vllm/model_executor/offloader/uva.py): each parameter is copied
to pinned host memory and replaced by `get_accelerator_view_from_cpu_tensor()`
-- a CUDA-addressable view of that host memory. The weights are never resident
in VRAM and are never staged in; CUDA kernels read them across PCIe during the
forward. Compute stays on the GPU; the CPU never runs the ViT. Weight loading
still works because wrapping happens at construction time, before load_weights
writes into the params -- the same ordering make_layers() already relies on.

Only the 27 transformer blocks are offloaded (0.77 of the tower's 0.86 GiB).
patch_embed, pos_embed, merger and deepstack_merger_list stay in VRAM: they are
small and, unlike the blocks, are not a clean repeating unit to hand to the
offloader.

Safe here because the ViT is neither torch.compile'd nor cudagraph'd in this
deployment (compile_mm_encoder and cudagraph_mm_encoder are both False). Do not
assume that holds if those are enabled.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
MEASURED (bench/vision_offload_ab.py, 5 unique images per size so neither the
mm-processor cache nor the prefix cache can serve a repeat; RTX 3090, PCIe 4.0
x16, Qwen3.5-27B W4A16, MTP spec decode on):

  KV pool         OFF: 5.17 GiB / 145,326 tok      ON: 5.95 GiB / 167,391 tok
                  (+0.78 GiB, +22,065 tokens, ~+29 blocks of 832)

  image     vis_tok    OFF (VRAM)   ON (UVA)    overhead
    224px        85       0.193s      0.493s      2.6x
    448px       217       0.456s      1.288s      2.8x
    896px       805       0.923s      2.536s      2.7x
   1280px      1621       1.335s      5.029s      3.8x

Why the cost is so much larger than a naive "one pass over 0.77 GiB at PCIe
bandwidth would be ~40 ms" estimate, and why it *scales with image size*
instead of being constant: GEMM kernels tile. A weight tile is re-read once
per row-block of the activation matrix, so a forward pass reads each weight
many times, not once -- e.g. a 1280px image is ~6,484 patches pre-merge, so
the (1152 x 3456) qkv weight is re-read on the order of 50x per layer. In VRAM
(~936 GB/s) those re-reads are nearly free; across PCIe (~20 GB/s effective)
they dominate. L2 (6 MB on a 3090) absorbs some but not most of it.

The general lesson: UVA weight offloading suits memory-bound, low-arithmetic-
intensity layers, not a compute-heavy ViT fed large activation matrices.

Choosing a value:
  - 0 (default): weights in VRAM. Fast images, ~29 fewer KV blocks.
  - 1: whole block stack offloaded. Max KV pool, slowest images.
  - Intermediate (e.g. 0.4): partial -- the offloader fills its budget
    per-parameter and stops, so roughly half the blocks are offloaded and the
    rest stay in VRAM. Untested here, but the mechanism supports it.
Capping --limit-mm-per-prompt's width/height also directly bounds the worst
case, since the overhead scales with patch count.

